---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import mapShot from "../images/dinosaurs/exploration-map.png";
import findShot from "../images/dinosaurs/occurrence-panel.png";
import treeShot from "../images/dinosaurs/taxonomy-browser.png";
import puzzleShot from "../images/dinosaurs/dinordle-puzzle.png";

const appUrl = "https://paleomap.nelson-j.ch/";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Dinosaurs · Nelson Jeanrenaud"
  description="A personal project: a map that shows where and when dinosaur fossils have actually been found, from about 250 million years ago to the end of the age of dinosaurs."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <span id="top"></span>

  <main class="wrap dino">
    <!-- ───────────────── Head ───────────────── -->
    <section class="section dino__head" aria-label="Dinosaurs">
      <p class="kicker">Side project</p>
      <h1 class="dino__title">A map of the dinosaurs</h1>
      <p class="lead dino__tagline">
        Where — and when — dinosaur fossils have actually been dug up, across
        the roughly 180 million years dinosaurs walked the Earth.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ───────────────── Hero screenshot ───────────────── -->
    <section class="section dino__hero" aria-label="A look at the map">
      <Image
        src={mapShot}
        alt="The map screen: an old world map with colourful dots marking dinosaur fossil discoveries, and a timeline slider running along the top."
        width={1400}
        loading="eager"
      />
      <p class="dino__caption">
        Slide through time and the map redraws the continents to match — this
        view is set to roughly 72 million years ago, not long before the
        asteroid.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ───────────────── Story ───────────────── -->
    <section class="section dino__body">
      <p>
        A while back I got curious about dinosaurs again — properly curious,
        not just "big lizard, roar." I wanted to know where they actually
        lived, and that turned out to be a surprisingly tricky question,
        because the continents themselves were in completely different places
        back then. The land was still drifting apart while dinosaurs were
        around, so a present-day map is the wrong map to use.
      </p>
      <p>
        So, as a hobby project, I built one that isn't. It's a map that
        redraws the continents the way scientists believe they looked at any
        point from about 250 million years ago up to the asteroid impact that
        ended the dinosaurs' reign, with real fossil discoveries plotted on
        top. Drag a slider and two things move together: the shape of the
        land, and the dots marking where dinosaurs have actually been found.
      </p>
      <p>
        I tried to build it to be honest rather than flashy. A dot marks a
        place a fossil was dug up — not a guess at everywhere that animal
        might have roamed, which is a different thing and easy to blur
        together without meaning to. And when something isn't known for sure,
        the map says so instead of quietly papering over the gap.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ───────────────── Gallery ───────────────── -->
    <section class="section dino__gallery" aria-label="More of the project">
      <p class="kicker">A closer look</p>
      <div class="dino__grid">
        <figure class="dino__item">
          <Image
            src={findShot}
            alt="A detail card open for a single fossil discovery, showing what it is, roughly when it lived, and where the information came from."
            width={900}
          />
          <figcaption>
            Click any dot to see what it is, roughly when it lived, and where
            that information comes from.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="dino__item">
          <Image
            src={treeShot}
            alt="A browsable dinosaur family tree, from broad groups down to individual species."
            width={900}
          />
          <figcaption>
            A family tree you can browse — from big groups like "armoured
            dinosaurs" down to individual species.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="dino__item">
          <Image
            src={puzzleShot}
            alt="A daily dinosaur-guessing puzzle called Dinordle, styled after the word game Wordle."
            width={900}
          />
          <figcaption>
            There's also a daily guessing game, Dinordle — like Wordle, but
            each guess narrows down a dinosaur instead of a word.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ───────────────── CTA ───────────────── -->
    <section class="section dino__cta">
      <a class="dino__link" href={appUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Explore the map ↗
      </a>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
